=base:main-content.cmark

# What tramaj is for

tramaj is a small functional language for composing values and documents. A
program evaluates against a JSON-like context and produces either an
ordinary value or a generic document tree — the host decides what to do with
it. That shape shows up in a few recurring situations.

## Turning JSON into markup, safely

The original motivation: templates that are comfortable to write by hand
*and* easy for an LLM to emit. A `jq`-flavoured expression half handles the
data, a HAML-like block syntax handles structure, and the builtin set stays
small enough that neither a person nor a model has to hold much in their
head to use it correctly. This site itself is one instance of that — [static
site pre-processing, the way Kitchen-Sink itself does it](/reference.html).

## Templating a real UI, not just markup

`tramaj-halogen` folds an evaluated `Node` into `Halogen.HTML`, wiring each
`action(...)` in the template to the host's own `Action` type. Because
actions are opaque structured data rather than host-specific event handlers,
a template can describe *what* should happen ("on-click, select, id: 42")
without knowing *how* the host will react to it — the same template could
drive a different UI framework by pairing it with a different fold.

## Documents an LLM can emit and a host can trust

Because imports, action keys, and context reads are all static positions —
literal strings, never computed — a host can run the static analyses in
[`Tramaj.Analysis`](/reference-language.html) over a model-generated template
*before* evaluating it: what does it import, what actions can it possibly
emit, what context does it read, which import parameters are still
unsupplied. That turns "an LLM wrote this template" from a trust problem
into an inspectable one. See [Properties](/properties.html) for why this
holds.

## Constraint-driven generation

v3 adds symbolic values (`?(key)`) and constraints (`!constraint(name,
...)`). Running evaluation in `Symbolic` mode produces an envelope of root
value, symbols, and constraints instead of a finished document — useful when
the actual values should come from a solver, a form the user hasn't filled
in yet, or a search process, rather than being supplied up front. See
[`v3-symbols.md`](/reference-v3-symbols.html).

## Typed template contracts

v4 adds a nominal type algebra on top of that: type declarations, type
parameters threaded through imports, and `@x : T = e` annotations that are
resolved and erased before evaluation. tramaj fixes what a type *is*
(identity is structural string equality over canonical ids); a separate host
checker decides whether the values flowing through a template actually
satisfy it. That's useful wherever a template is a contract between two
teams or two systems — e.g. a component library whose consumers should be
checked against its declared shape. See [`v4-types.md`](/reference-v4-types.html).

## Try it

The [playground](/playground.html) runs all of the above in the browser —
tabs-as-importable-libraries, live AST/render panels, an action log, and a
symbolic-mode toggle — with nothing sent to a server.

